## Gather the brief

Run a short sequential interview with one question per turn. Ask for open-ended information in plain conversation. Use `AskUserQuestion` in Claude Code or `request_user_input` in Codex only for bounded choices with useful predefined options, such as product language, layout direction, or optional integrations. Do not use a question tool when the expected answer is primarily free text, and do not invent choices when context is missing. Wait for the answer, record it and every safely inferred field in `SETUP.md`, then continue. Skip information already supplied or recorded. Keep prompts conversational and concise; do not label them `Question X of Y` or reveal the full questionnaire.

When setup starts with a brief (text the user wrote alongside `/z2a`, `$z2a`, `/setup`, or `$setup`), extract everything it settles before asking anything:

- Product name: use the name as written. If it is entirely lowercase, title-case each word (`open student` becomes `Open Student`). Otherwise keep the user's casing (`OpenStudent` stays `OpenStudent`).
- Description: the brief's description of what the product does, as one sentence.
- Primary locale and direction: the language the brief is written in. A Hebrew brief means `he` and `rtl`. An English brief means `en` and `ltr`. Ask the language question only when the brief names a different product language than the one it is written in, or has too little text to tell.
- Requested features: only the features the brief explicitly asks to build, such as a list of screens, flows, or capabilities. Record them as a short list in the brief's words. Record `None` when the brief gives only a name, a description, a language, or a direction. Never infer features from the product name, description, or category.

Ask for the missing decisions in this order:

1. In plain conversation, ask for the product name and a one-sentence description of what it should do. Record those words directly and continue.
{{#mobile}}
2. Unless the brief already settled the primary locale, use the question tool to ask whether the app's primary language is Hebrew, English, or a different language. Record Hebrew as `he` and English as `en`. If the user chooses a different language, ask for its language or locale code in plain conversation, then record a valid BCP 47 code; accept additional locales when the user supplies them. Treat the first locale as the app's primary language. A primary `he` locale means a Hebrew UI and `rtl` direction automatically; do not ask a separate direction question. For other locales, infer the standard direction and use the question tool only when the direction is genuinely ambiguous.
{{/mobile}}
{{#web}}
2. Unless the brief already settled the primary locale, use the question tool to ask whether the app's primary language is Hebrew, English, or a different language. Record Hebrew as `he` and English as `en`. If the user chooses a different language, ask for its language or locale code in plain conversation, then record a valid BCP 47 code; accept additional locales when the user supplies them. Treat the first locale as the app's primary language. Infer its standard layout direction and use the question tool for direction (`ltr`, `rtl`, or `auto`) only when the direction is genuinely ambiguous.
{{/web}}
3. Use the question tool to offer Neon MCP, PostHog MCP, and Resend MCP as independent optional decisions. Give each choice a short purpose: Neon provides the product database, PostHog provides analytics, and Resend provides email for flows such as payment confirmations, receipts, sign-in links, and other transactional messages. Explain briefly that configuration is project-scoped and authentication remains a user action.
   Each accepted integration also gets working code after initialization (see Wire integrations), so mention that accepting one means the product can use it right away.
{{#mobile}}
   Also say, in one sentence, that the database and email are reached only through the app's own server routes, so accepting either also sets those up and keeps keys out of the app.
{{/mobile}}

{{#mobile}}
Derive the lowercase npm package name, Expo slug, URI scheme, and both native identifiers from the product name. Use `com.<normalized-slug>` for native identifiers and prefix the slug segment with `app` if it would not begin with a letter. Never ask for package naming, a URI scheme, or a reverse-domain namespace. Generate an accessible five-color palette that fits the product description and record brand assets as deferred because none are expected during setup; do not ask about colors or assets. Apply and verify these assumptions. Use another provider only when explicitly requested. Ask a follow-up only when the missing answer would materially change the product and cannot safely be inferred.

Setup establishes identity and foundations; it does not define the product. Do not generate or present a synthesized brief, audience, problem, outcome, feature list, roadmap, or implementation plan. Preserve details the user volunteers. Build product features only when they are recorded under `Requested features` (see Build requested features); otherwise leave product development for the next task.
{{/mobile}}
{{#web}}
Derive the lowercase npm package name from the product name and never ask the user to choose it. Generate an accessible five-color palette that fits the product description; do not ask about colors. Record and apply primary, accent, background, surface, and text colors, then verify contrast. Use another provider only when the user explicitly requests it. Ask a follow-up only when the missing answer would materially change the product and cannot safely be inferred.
{{/web}}

{{#web}}
Setup establishes identity, foundations, and a coherent first-pass landing page; it does not finalize the product. Generate provisional positioning and section copy from the user's name and description, using conservative implications only. Keep unsupported features, audiences, outcomes, metrics, prices, testimonials, and marketing claims out of `PRODUCT.md` and do not present inferred details as settled decisions. Features recorded under `Requested features` are supported: build them after initialization (see Build requested features); otherwise leave product development for the next task.
{{/web}}

## Preserve the template layout

Setup renames and configures the template; it never redesigns or strips it. Change only identity, colors, locale, direction, and visible copy.

When the primary locale is `he`, open the bundled `hebrew-copy` skill (`.claude/skills/hebrew-copy/SKILL.md` in Claude Code, `.agents/skills/hebrew-copy/SKILL.md` in Codex) before writing any copy, and follow its voice, glossary, and punctuation.

{{#mobile}}
- Keep every route, screen, tab, component, and style in `src/app` and `src/components`. Do not delete, merge, reorder, or replace screens with a minimal shell.
- In the home screen, show the product name in the brand badge, use the product name or description as the title, and the description as the subtitle. Replace the starter hint rows, including the `npm run reset-project` row that no longer applies, with neutral placeholder rows in the same structure.
- Translate tab labels, settings labels, and all other starter-owned visible copy into the primary product locale.
{{/mobile}}
{{#web}}
- Keep every section, component, class name, icon, visual element, and style in `app/page.tsx` and `app/globals.css`. Do not delete, merge, reorder, or replace sections with a minimal shell.
- Rewrite every value in `app/landing-content.ts` and remove its `SETUP:` comment. Use the product name and supplied description as factual anchors, then generate natural contextual draft copy for every other field based on what the user wrote.
- Replace hard-coded visible text in `app/page.tsx` (navigation labels, `aria-label` text, section kickers, the CTA eyebrow, and the hero dashboard mock) with contextual draft copy in the primary product locale. Replace the `0→` brand mark with the product's initial.
{{/web}}
{{#mobile}}
- Placeholder copy is plainly labeled for replacement, written in the primary product locale, and never pretends to be real: use obvious placeholders such as "Feature one — describe the benefit here", not invented features, benefits, prices, metrics, user counts, customer names, or testimonials. Stats and testimonials stay as clearly labeled placeholders.
{{/mobile}}
{{#web}}
- Contextual draft copy should read like a plausible first version of this specific product, not generic labels such as "Feature one", "placeholder", "coming soon", or "replace this text". Infer useful wording from the supplied description without inventing facts. Keep numbers non-quantitative, make any testimonial-shaped section an aspirational product statement rather than a fabricated customer quote, and use CTA labels and links that are safe before the real product flow exists.
{{/web}}
- Translate the supplied one-sentence description into the primary product locale when needed without asking another question. Product names remain unchanged unless the user supplied a localized name.


